      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4 leading-tight">
        Tech Workers and BC PNP in 2027: Your Real PR Options Now That Tech Draws Have Ended
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-4">
        For years, B.C. ran weekly tech draws with low cutoffs. That era is over, and the federal STEM category no longer covers most software roles. Here is what a developer, data or IT professional in B.C. can still use, and how to choose.
      </p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <time datetime="2026-10-14">October 14, 2026</time>
        <span>&bull;</span>
        <span>12 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-10">
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-2xl font-black text-sky-600 mb-2">Dec 3, 2024</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Last BC PNP priority tech draw</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-indigo-600 mb-2">$52.40</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">B.C. median hourly wage, software developers (Job Bank)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-red-600 mb-2">0</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Express Entry STEM draws, Jan to mid-Oct 2026</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        If you work in tech in British Columbia, you may still be reading advice that assumes the BC PNP runs dedicated technology draws. It does not. The BC PNP's April 23, 2026 update made it official: "The final draw for BC PNP priority technology occupations occurred on December 3, 2024." Tech is not on the new Care or Build lists either. That does not mean tech workers are shut out, but it does mean the strategy has changed. This guide sets out every realistic route, with the numbers you need to decide between them.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What Changed, and What Did Not</h2>

      <p>The April 23, 2026 announcement contained three sentences that matter for tech workers:</p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li>The last priority tech draw was December 3, 2024.</li>
        <li>"Going forward, the BC PNP will continue to issue targeted invitations for individuals creating high economic impact in B.C. to capture top talent across all sectors, including technology."</li>
        <li>"All occupations on the previous technology occupations list remain eligible for the BC PNP."</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        So your occupation is still eligible for the Skilled Worker stream. What disappeared is the <em>priority</em>: a separate draw where tech candidates competed only against each other. Today, a developer competes in the same High Economic Impact rounds as engineers, managers and finance professionals.
      </p>

      <p>
        One tech-friendly rule survived. Job offers normally must be indeterminate, but Appendix A of the Skills Immigration Program Guide lists occupations that can qualify with a limited-term job offer. Its "Technology" group has 35 occupations, including 20012 computer and information systems managers, 21211 data scientists, 21220 cybersecurity specialists, 21230 to 21234 (systems developers, software engineers, software developers, web designers, web developers), 22220 to 22222 (network, user support and testing technicians), several engineering NOCs and some film and media occupations. The offer must be at least 365 days long, with at least 120 calendar days remaining when you apply. That matters for contractors on fixed-term project work.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Option 1: BC PNP High Economic Impact Invitations</h2>

      <p>
        High Economic Impact rounds are now the BC PNP's main door for tech. In 2026 each round had a wage lane and a points lane. The September 24, 2026 round invited 426 candidates earning at least $52 an hour and $105,000 a year in a TEER 0 to 3 job, plus 288 candidates with a score of 131 or more. Earlier rounds were tougher: the wage line was $70 an hour in February. Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-innovate-high-economic-impact-draws-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">High Economic Impact analysis</a> lists every 2026 round.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Where tech pay sits against the wage line</h3>

      <p>Job Bank's wage data for software developers and programmers (NOC 21232) in B.C. (2023 to 2024 survey data, updated November 19, 2025):</p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Region</th>
              <th class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Low</th>
              <th class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Median</th>
              <th class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">High</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-6 py-3">British Columbia</td><td class="px-6 py-3">$31.25</td><td class="px-6 py-3">$52.40</td><td class="px-6 py-3">$84.13</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-6 py-3">Lower Mainland&ndash;Southwest</td><td class="px-6 py-3">$32.00</td><td class="px-6 py-3">$52.40</td><td class="px-6 py-3">$84.13</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-6 py-3">Vancouver Island and Coast</td><td class="px-6 py-3">$28.00</td><td class="px-6 py-3">$51.28</td><td class="px-6 py-3">$79.33</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-6 py-3">Kootenay</td><td class="px-6 py-3">$33.22</td><td class="px-6 py-3">$47.16</td><td class="px-6 py-3">$69.79</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-6 py-3">Thompson&ndash;Okanagan</td><td class="px-6 py-3">$32.48</td><td class="px-6 py-3">$46.76</td><td class="px-6 py-3">$69.05</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The September wage line of $52 sat almost exactly at the B.C. median. A mid-career developer at or above the median could have been invited on wage alone; a junior developer could not. Remember that the BC PNP counts only base pay. Bonuses, commissions and overtime are excluded, so a total-compensation package that relies on bonuses may look strong on paper and still miss the line.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">If your pay is below the line: the points lane</h3>

      <p>
        Points cutoffs were 131 to 138 in 2026. Metro Vancouver jobs earn zero area points, so reaching 131 there typically takes near-maximum education, language and experience points plus a high wage. For example, a B.C. master's degree (30), CLB 9 (30), full experience points (40) and $45 an hour (30) add up to 130. A job in Area 3 (anywhere outside Metro Vancouver and the five Area 2 communities of Squamish, Abbotsford, Agassiz, Mission and Chilliwack) earns 15 points, plus 10 more for a year of regional work experience or for graduating from a public institution outside Metro Vancouver. For many tech workers, a regional role at Victoria, Kelowna or Kamloops pay is worth more points than a pay rise in Vancouver. Run your own numbers in the <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP calculator</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Option 2: Express Entry, Canadian Experience Class</h2>

      <p>
        For tech workers already in Canada on a work permit, the Canadian Experience Class (CEC) has been the most active federal route in 2026. IRCC's rounds data shows CEC-only draws roughly every two weeks, with lowest CRS scores between 507 and 523 and round sizes from 1,000 to 8,000 invitations.
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Experience:</strong> at least one year (1,560 hours) of skilled Canadian work in TEER 0 to 3 within the three years before you apply.</li>
        <li><strong>What does not count:</strong> work while studying full-time, co-op terms, and self-employment (with a narrow exception for some physicians).</li>
        <li><strong>Language:</strong> CLB 7 in all four abilities for TEER 0 or 1 jobs (most developer, engineer and manager roles); CLB 5 for TEER 2 or 3. Results must be less than two years old.</li>
        <li><strong>Education:</strong> none required for CEC, though it adds CRS points.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        A score above 507 usually needs high language scores plus Canadian experience and a degree. If you are short, a provincial nomination adds enough CRS points to virtually guarantee an invitation, which is why pairing CEC with a BC PNP registration under the <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-express-entry-bc-eebc-guide-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Express Entry BC option</a> makes sense. IRCC's provincial nominee draws in 2026 had lowest scores between 697 and 805, which reflects the nomination bonus.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-amber-900 mb-2">If you are outside Canada</p>
        <p class="text-amber-800">In IRCC's 2026 rounds data through October 13, every draw was either a CEC draw, a provincial nominee draw or a category draw. We found no all-program or Federal Skilled Worker-only rounds. A tech worker abroad without Canadian experience, French or a provincial nomination has had very few federal invitations to compete for this year.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Option 3: Express Entry STEM Category, and Why It Rarely Helps Developers</h2>

      <p>
        Many tech workers assume they are "STEM." In IRCC's terms, most are not. The 2026 STEM category on canada.ca lists architecture and science managers (20011), cybersecurity specialists (21220), civil, mechanical, electrical and electronics, industrial and manufacturing, and geological engineers, and three engineering technologist groups, among a short list. It does <strong>not</strong> include software developers (21232), software engineers (21231), computer systems developers (21230), data scientists (21211), information systems specialists (21222) or computer and information systems managers (20012).
      </p>

      <p>
        Even for those who are on the list, the category has not been used this year: IRCC's rounds data shows no STEM draw between January 1 and October 13, 2026. Eligibility also requires 12 months of full-time work in one listed occupation within the past three years. Treat STEM eligibility as a bonus, not a plan.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Option 4: Categories Some Tech Workers Can Use</h2>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>French-language proficiency.</strong> Requires NCLC 7 in all four French abilities. In 2026, French category draws had lowest CRS scores between 382 and 420, far below CEC. For a tech worker with strong French, this is the single biggest federal advantage available.</li>
        <li><strong>Senior managers with Canadian work experience.</strong> Requires 12 months in Canada in one of the senior manager NOCs 00012 to 00015. Rounds on March 5, July 10 and September 16, 2026 had lowest scores of 429, 392 and 389. This is for executives, not team leads: computer and information systems managers (20012) are not on the list.</li>
        <li><strong>Researchers with Canadian work experience.</strong> Limited to university professors and lecturers (41200) and post-secondary teaching and research assistants (41201). Useful for academic AI and computer science researchers, not industry engineers.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Option 5: Other Provinces</h2>

      <p>Every province has its own rules, and a nomination usually requires you to intend to live there. Two developments matter for tech workers:</p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Alberta.</strong> The Alberta Express Entry stream has an Accelerated Tech Pathway for candidates with "a job offer for an eligible tech occupation from an Alberta employer in an eligible tech industry," with expedited processing. You need an active Express Entry profile and a qualifying Alberta offer. See our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-vs-alberta-aaip-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP vs Alberta comparison</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Ontario.</strong> Ontario's immigrant nominee program page says the new Ontario Workforce Priority stream has launched and all other streams are now closed. If you were counting on an Ontario stream that did not require a job offer, check the current requirements on ontario.ca before you move.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Option 6: Staying in Status While You Qualify</h2>

      <p>
        Permanent residence often takes longer than a work permit lasts. Two federal tools help tech workers stay employed:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Global Talent Stream.</strong> An employer-driven Labour Market Impact Assessment with a 10-business-day service standard (80 per cent of the time) and two-week work permit processing (also 80 per cent of the time). Category B covers in-demand, highly skilled occupations on the Global Talent occupations list. It gives a work permit, not permanent residence, but it can keep you working long enough to build CEC experience.</li>
        <li><strong>BC PNP work permit support.</strong> Once nominated, the BC PNP may issue a work permit support letter so you can obtain or renew a work permit without an LMIA, as long as you continue to meet the conditions of nomination.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Two announced federal measures are worth knowing about but not relying on. Budget 2025 said "in the coming months the government will also launch an accelerated pathway for H1-B visa holders." At the time of writing we could not find eligibility criteria or an application process on canada.ca. Budget 2025 also announced a one-time measure to accelerate permanent residence for up to 33,000 work permit holders; IRCC's May 4, 2026 release says it applies to people who have already applied through programs such as the PNP and have lived in smaller communities for two years or more, and that no new application stream is being created. More detail is in our <a href="/articles/canada-tr-to-pr-pathway-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">TR to PR pathway guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Which Route Fits Your Profile</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Your situation</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Strongest first move</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Backup</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">In B.C., base pay at or above about $52/hr, TEER 0 to 3</td><td class="px-4 py-3">BC PNP registration (EEBC option if eligible), aiming for the wage lane</td><td class="px-4 py-3">CEC if CRS is competitive</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">In B.C. on a PGWP, under $52/hr, 1+ year Canadian experience</td><td class="px-4 py-3">CEC, after raising language scores</td><td class="px-4 py-3">BC PNP points lane, stronger with a regional job</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Strong French (NCLC 7+)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Express Entry French category</td><td class="px-4 py-3">CEC and BC PNP in parallel</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Fixed-term tech contract in B.C.</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Check Appendix A: a 1-year+ offer with 120+ days left may qualify</td><td class="px-4 py-3">CEC</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Outside Canada, no Canadian experience</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Employer-sponsored work permit (for example Global Talent Stream) to build Canadian experience</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Provinces with tech pathways that accept your job offer</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Cybersecurity specialist or engineer on the STEM list</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Same as your wage and experience profile above</td><td class="px-4 py-3">STEM category if IRCC resumes STEM draws</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What to Expect in 2027 (Analysis)</h2>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>No return of BC tech draws is announced.</strong> The BC PNP has framed tech as part of Innovate. If past patterns hold, tech workers will keep competing in mixed High Economic Impact rounds.</li>
        <li><strong>Federal "top talent" may get more attention.</strong> IRCC's 2026 consultation on 2027 Express Entry categories (August 4 to September 1, 2026) said it is "considering expanding our approach to attract and retain highly-skilled international workers," while also "considering narrowing the number or eligibility" of labour-shortage categories. No decisions have been announced, and the consultation page links AI talent to expanding the Global Talent Stream rather than to a new Express Entry category.</li>
        <li><strong>Temporary resident reductions continue.</strong> IRCC's goal is to bring temporary residents below 5 per cent of the population by the end of 2027. For tech workers on PGWPs, that makes planning around permit expiry dates more important, not less.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Waiting for a tech draw.</strong> There is none scheduled. Register now if you qualify for High Economic Impact.</li>
        <li><strong>Assuming you are in the STEM category.</strong> Check the NOC list on canada.ca. Most software NOCs are not on it.</li>
        <li><strong>Counting total compensation.</strong> Stock and bonuses do not count toward BC PNP wage points or the wage lane.</li>
        <li><strong>Choosing the wrong NOC.</strong> Your NOC must match your actual duties. A developer registered as a manager to look more senior risks refusal for misrepresentation.</li>
        <li><strong>Letting language results expire.</strong> BC PNP and Express Entry both need results valid at registration or profile creation and at application.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-3 not-prose">
        <details class="group border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-4">
          <summary class="font-semibold text-slate-900 cursor-pointer">Does the BC PNP still hold tech draws?</summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700">No. The BC PNP says its final draw for priority technology occupations took place on December 3, 2024. It also says all occupations on the previous technology list remain eligible, and that it will continue High Economic Impact invitations to capture top talent across all sectors, including technology.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="group border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-4">
          <summary class="font-semibold text-slate-900 cursor-pointer">Are software developers in the Express Entry STEM category in 2026?</summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700">No. IRCC's 2026 STEM category list includes occupations such as cybersecurity specialists, several engineering occupations and engineering technologists, but not software developers and programmers (NOC 21232), software engineers (21231), computer systems developers (21230) or data scientists (21211). IRCC's rounds data also shows no STEM category draw between January and mid-October 2026.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="group border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-4">
          <summary class="font-semibold text-slate-900 cursor-pointer">Can a tech worker on a contract qualify for the BC PNP?</summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700">Possibly. BC PNP job offers normally must be indeterminate, but Appendix A of the Skills Immigration Program Guide lists technology occupations that may qualify with a limited-term job offer if the offer is at least one year long and at least 120 calendar days remain when you apply.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="group border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-4">
          <summary class="font-semibold text-slate-900 cursor-pointer">What CRS score did Canadian Experience Class draws need in 2026?</summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700">IRCC's rounds data shows Canadian Experience Class draws in 2026 with lowest CRS scores between 507 and 523. Cutoffs change with every round.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="group border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-4">
          <summary class="font-semibold text-slate-900 cursor-pointer">Has Canada launched the H-1B pathway to permanent residence?</summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700">Budget 2025 said the government would launch an accelerated pathway for H-1B visa holders in the coming months. At the time of writing we could not find published eligibility criteria or an application process on canada.ca, so do not plan around it until IRCC publishes details.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

